  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Question II. 4) "Gender and patriarchy are also known to have a great influence in perpetuating the existence of educational inequality." Explain the statement with an example of your own. (5 marks)', 'In patriarchal societies, men hold more power and boys'' education is often seen as more valuable because sons are expected to earn and lead, while daughters are expected to marry and do housework. As a result girls are enrolled less, leave school earlier and have fewer chances of higher education, and this inequality is passed to the next generation. For example, in a poor rural family with limited money, the parents may send their son to a boarding school in town while keeping their daughter at home to look after younger siblings and the farm; years later, the son finds a salaried job and the daughter does not, repeating the same pattern for her own children.', null, '[{"point": "Explanation: patriarchal values favour boys/ men, limiting girls'' access, continuation and attainment; reproduce inequality across generations", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Own relevant example (e.g. girls kept home for chores/ early marriage, families investing only in sons, lack of safe hostels/ toilets, stereotypes in subject choice)", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'english-language'),
